1 In order to preserve professional confidentiality, self-regulatory organisations shall arrange for inspections under this Act (AMLA inspections) to be carried out on lawyers by lawyers and on notaries by notaries.
2 The lawyers and notaries instructed to carry out AMLA inspections must meet the following requirements:
a. lawyer’s or notary’s practising certificate;
b. guarantee of that inspections will be carried out properly;
c. proof of the relevant knowledge of AMLA, practical experience and continuing professional development;
d. independence from the person affiliated to the self-regulatory organisation who is being inspected.
3 Where there are objective grounds to suspect a violation of the duty of due diligence, it is absolutely necessary for the purposes of the inspections, and professional confidentiality has been waived by a court or by the customer, lawyers and notaries must disclose information subject to professional confidentiality to the lawyers and notaries responsible for carrying out the AMLA inspections. The self-regulatory organisations shall lay down objective criteria for deciding whether duties of due diligence have been violated.
4 Subject to paragraph 3, lawyers and notaries responsible for carrying out AMLA inspections shall not have access to information protected by professional confidentiality. They shall not disclose information made available following the lifting of professional confidentiality to either the self-regulatory organisations or other authorities, and must not include such information in any report to the self-regulatory organisation on the inspection. They shall ensure that information and documents obtained during the inspection are held in safekeeping and that professional confidentiality is maintained.
5 The compulsory measures court at the place where the adviser is based has jurisdiction to lift professional confidentiality.
6 Where a notary is also subject to supervision by a cantonal authority pursuant to cantonal law, and the appointed person meets both the requirements set out in paragraph 2 and those of cantonal law, the supervision may be coordinated.

1 Organisations are recognised as self-regulatory organisations if they:
a. have regulations in accordance with Article 25;
b. supervise the persons affiliated to them with regard to compliance with their duties in terms of Chapter 2;
c. provide the guarantee of irreproachable business conduct and ensure that the persons and audit firms they instruct to carry out inspections:1.
possess the required specialist knowledge,
2. provide the required guarantees that inspections will be carried out properly, and
3. are independent of the management and administration of financial intermediaries being inspected;
d. ensure that the audit firms they instruct to carry out inspections and lead auditors fulfil the requirements under Article 24a.
2 The self-regulatory organisations of the licensed transport undertakings under the Public Transport Act of 20 March 2009 must be independent of their respective managements.

1 Self-regulatory organisations must issue regulations.
2 The regulations shall specify the duties of diligence of affiliated persons in accordance with Chapter 2 and stipulate how these duties must be fulfilled.
3 They shall further stipulate:
a. the requirements for the affiliation and exclusion of persons while upholding the principles of equal treatment and economic freedom;
b. how compliance with the duties in terms of Chapter 2 is monitored;
c. effective, appropriate and proportionate sanctions in the form of warnings, reprimands and contractual penalties, whereby the self-regulatory organisations shall agree among themselves on a uniform framework for sanctions, including maximum amounts;
d. the right to challenge decisions before the standing arbitral tribunal pursuant to Article 25a.

